This repository contains the training pipeline and final weights for a highly optimized YOLOv8s object detection model designed to classify dental diseases in panoramic X-rays. Engineered by Abdullah Tarek Habashy, a recent Alexandria University Computer and Data Science graduate, the project merges the Tufts Dental Database with specialized caries datasets to establish a unified taxonomy. The primary focus was overcoming severe dataset annotation artifacts to achieve accurate, lesion-level detection for elusive features like cavities.

The DENTEX Annotation Problem
The most significant engineering hurdle occurred during the initial dataset integration.

Initial attempts utilized the DENTEX Challenge 2023 dataset to train the Caries and Deep Caries classes.

The model hit a hard plateau, persistently scoring 0% Recall on cavities despite aggressive Focal Loss and penalty tuning.

Visual diagnostic scripts revealed that DENTEX bounding boxes were "tooth-level" rather than "lesion-level," encompassing massive areas of healthy tissue.

Because the boxes were predominantly healthy enamel, YOLO mathematically treated the micro-features of actual decay as background noise.

Dataset Pivot & Fine-Tuning Strategy
To cure the poisoned training pool, a two-phase training strategy was deployed alongside a complete dataset overhaul.

DENTEX was completely purged and replaced with the Loki Silvres dataset to inject surgically tight, lesion-specific annotations.

Phase 1 utilized moderate mosaic augmentation (mosaic=0.5) to break the 0% plateau, pushing Caries Recall to 60.0%.

Phase 2 fine-tuned the checkpoint for 35 epochs with early stopping disabled (patience=0) and a lowered learning rate (lr0=0.002).

Mosaic augmentations were disabled (mosaic=0.0) to allow the network to evaluate teeth at their native scale and tighten bounding box precision.

Final Checkpoint Results
The dataset pivot and refinement strategy resolved the small-object detection failures.

Caries Recall reached 66.7%, proving the network successfully learned to identify the micro-features of dental decay.

Caries mAP50 soared to 64.8%, alongside a strong Precision score of 0.555.

The overall multi-class mAP50 stabilized at 38.0%.

The optimized best.pt weights have been committed to the repository for inference and deployment.
